  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 203 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nThe potential for more active weather is still on track to arrive \nSaturday as a trough digs southward across the High Plains and into \nthe central US. Confidence in this system and precipitation timing \nand development continues to increase, although, precipitation type \nis still on the lower end. Temperatures are expected to be warm \nenough to support all rain for most of the area during the morning \nhours. As colder air begins to push into northwest Nebraska, areas \nacross the Panhandle and Pine Ridge could see some rain/snow mix by \nearly afternoon, changing over to all snow by late afternoon. The \nchangeover of rain to snow continues to depend highly on the speed \nof the colder air behind the trough versus how fast the \nprecipitation exits the area. If that colder air can push in \nquicker, not only snow could be possible further to the east across \nthe Sandhills and into north central Nebraska, but some higher \naccumulations could be possible across the Pine Ridge. Since \nconfidence still isn't high on the changeover times, accumulations \nacross the area are uncertain at this time. What can be said though, \nis the latest probabilities show the potential of receiving 0.1 inch \nof snowfall to be near 80 percent across Sheridan and western Cherry \ncounty and the probability of 0.5 inch of snowfall to be near 20 \npercent which is fairly consistent with previous forecasts. Another \nconsideration with this system is that even if rain can switch over \nto snow early enough, snow falling on wet pavement or ground will be \nslow to accumulate. Therefore, don't feel confident advertising \nanything more than some minor, potentially slushy, accumulations at \nthis time. A dry and quiet forecast will return on Sunday lasting \ninto mid-week as upper level ridging builds back across the western \nUS and surface high pressure returns over the Great Plains. \n\nTemperatures will range from the upper 30s in the Pine Ridge to the \nupper 40s south of I-80 on Saturday as cold air advection begins to \npush southward into Nebraska. Temperatures will even dip into the \nlow to mid 40s on Sunday as the colder air behind the departing \ntrough filters across the region. Highs return to the mid 40s to mid \n50s through the middle of the week as ridging returns. Overnight \nlows will generally remain above normals through Wednesday night. \nExpect lows in the mid 20s to mid 30s which is nearly 5 to 10 \ndegrees above normals (upper teens to low 20s).",
